\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained development of the logarithmic negativity $\EN(\rho)=\log\|\Gamma\rho\|_1$
of a bipartite quantum state, where $\Gamma$ is partial transposition on the second tensor factor and
$\|\cdot\|_1$ is the trace norm. The development is organised around a \emph{variational} definition of
the trace norm of a Hermitian matrix,
$\|X\|_1=\inf\{\tr P+\tr Q: X=P-Q,\ P,Q\succeq0\}$,
together with its dual description as a supremum of $\Rem\tr(XW)$ over Hermitian contractions
$-\Id\preceq W\preceq\Id$. We prove that primal infimum and dual supremum are both attained --- by the
spectral Jordan pair and by the sign operator of the spectrum --- and that they coincide with
$\sum_i|\lambda_i|$, so that there is no duality gap. From this base we obtain contractivity of the
trace norm under positive trace-preserving maps; monotonicity of $\EN$ and of the negativity
$\Neg(\rho)=(\|\Gamma\rho\|_1-1)/2$ under PPT operations, hence under all local operations with
classical communication; strong (selective) monotonicity $\sum_i p_i \EN(\rho_i)\le\EN(\rho)$;
convexity of $\Neg$; faithfulness on the PPT class, $\EN(\rho)=0\iff\Gamma\rho\succeq0$, with vanishing
on all separable states; the dimension bound $\EN(\rho)\le\frac12\log(d_Ad_B)$ and its saturation
$\EN(\Phi_d)=\log d$; exact multiplicativity $\|A\otimes B\|_1=\|A\|_1\|B\|_1$ for Hermitian $A,B$ and
hence exact additivity $\EN(\rho\otimes\sigma)=\EN(\rho)+\EN(\sigma)$, contrasted with the
non-additive law $\Neg(\rho\otimes\sigma)=2\Neg(\rho)\Neg(\sigma)+\Neg(\rho)+\Neg(\sigma)$; and,
as applications, exact-distillation bounds and the impossibility of distilling a maximally entangled
state from a PPT state by PPT protocols. The methodological point is that the variational definition
makes monotonicity nearly automatic, while strong duality is exactly the extra input needed to upgrade
submultiplicativity of the trace norm to multiplicativity, and hence subadditivity of $\EN$ to additivity.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} logarithmic negativity; entanglement monotone; partial transpose; PPT
operations; trace norm; semidefinite duality; bound entanglement.

\section{Introduction}

Entanglement is a resource: it can be consumed to teleport states, to establish secret keys, and to
beat classical bounds in distributed computation, and it cannot be created for free by parties
restricted to local operations and classical communication (LOCC). A \emph{measure} of entanglement is
a function on bipartite states quantifying this resource, and the minimal requirement making such a
function a \emph{monotone} is that no LOCC protocol may increase it.

Many candidate measures are defined by optimisations over decompositions or extensions of the state and
are consequently hard to compute. The \emph{logarithmic negativity} stands out because it is given by an
explicit spectral formula, is computable by diagonalising a single matrix, is additive on tensor
products, and still satisfies monotonicity. The price is that it is not faithful on the
entangled/separable divide: it fails to detect bound entangled states, whose partial transposes are
positive.

\subsection{Setting and notation}

All systems are finite dimensional. $A$ and $B$ denote quantum systems with distinguished bases indexed
by finite sets of cardinalities $d_A$ and $d_B$; operators on $\HH_A\otimes\HH_B$ are matrices indexed
by pairs, with entries $X_{(i,j),(k,l)}$. We write $X^{\dagger}$ for the conjugate transpose, $X^{T}$
for the transpose, $\bar X$ for the entrywise conjugate, $\Id$ for the identity, and $X\succeq0$ for
positive semidefiniteness (which includes Hermiticity). A \emph{state} is a matrix $\rho\succeq0$ with
$\tr\rho=1$.

\begin{lemma}[Positivity toolbox]\label{lem:tools}
Let $P,Q\succeq0$ be of the same size. Then $\tr P\ge0$ and $\tr(PQ)\ge0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first is the sum of the eigenvalues. For the second, write $P=P^{1/2}P^{1/2}$ with $P^{1/2}\succeq0$;
by cyclicity $\tr(PQ)=\tr(P^{1/2}QP^{1/2})$, and $P^{1/2}QP^{1/2}=(P^{1/2})^{\dagger}QP^{1/2}\succeq0$
because congruence preserves positivity.
\end{proof}

\section{The trace norm, defined variationally}

\begin{definition}[Jordan pair]
A pair $(P,Q)$ is a \emph{Jordan pair} for a matrix $X$ if $P\succeq0$, $Q\succeq0$ and $X=P-Q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Existence]\label{prop:jordan}
Every Hermitian $X$ admits a Jordan pair. If $X=U\diag(\lambda)U^{\dagger}$ is a spectral decomposition,
then $P=U\diag(\max(\lambda_i,0))U^{\dagger}$ and $Q=U\diag(\max(-\lambda_i,0))U^{\dagger}$ form the
\emph{spectral Jordan pair}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Unitary conjugation of a diagonal matrix with non-negative entries is positive semidefinite. Since
$\max(t,0)-\max(-t,0)=t$ for real $t$, the difference of the two diagonal matrices is $\diag(\lambda)$;
conjugating by $U$ gives $P-Q=X$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Trace norm]
For any matrix $X$,
\[
\|X\|_1=\inf\bigl\{\Rem(\tr P+\tr Q)\ :\ (P,Q)\ \text{a Jordan pair for }X\bigr\},
\]
with the convention $\inf\emptyset=0$ (so $\|X\|_1=0$ for non-Hermitian $X$, a case never used).
\end{definition}

By Proposition~\ref{prop:jordan} the feasible set is non-empty for Hermitian $X$, and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:tools} it is bounded below by $0$, so the infimum is well defined and $\|X\|_1\ge0$.
Any Jordan pair yields an upper bound on $\|X\|_1$; lower bounds require a statement valid for
\emph{every} Jordan pair.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:basic}
Let $X,Y$ be Hermitian and $c\ge0$ real.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $P\succeq0$ then $\|P\|_1=\tr P$.
\item $\Rem\tr X\le\|X\|_1$.
\item $\|X+Y\|_1\le\|X\|_1+\|Y\|_1$, and $\bigl\|\sum_iX_i\bigr\|_1\le\sum_i\|X_i\|_1$ for finite
families of Hermitian matrices.
\item $\|cX\|_1=c\|X\|_1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(1) $(P,0)$ is Jordan, giving $\le$; conversely for any Jordan pair $(A,B)$ of $P$,
$\tr P=\tr A-\tr B\le\tr A+\tr B$ since $\tr B\ge0$.
(2) $\tr X=\tr P-\tr Q\le\tr P+\tr Q$ for any Jordan pair.
(3) If $(P_1,Q_1)$ and $(P_2,Q_2)$ are Jordan for $X$ and $Y$, then $(P_1+P_2,Q_1+Q_2)$ is Jordan for
$X+Y$ with the sum of the costs; optimise the two families successively. Induct for finite families.
(4) For $c>0$, scaling by $c$ is a cost-scaling bijection between Jordan pairs of $X$ and of $cX$;
applying the same with $c^{-1}$ gives the reverse inequality. The case $c=0$ is trivial.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Contractivity}

\begin{definition}
A map $\Phi$ on matrices is \emph{positive trace-preserving} (PTP) if $\Phi(A-B)=\Phi A-\Phi B$, if
$A\succeq0$ implies $\Phi A\succeq0$, and if $\tr\Phi A=\tr A$ for $A\succeq0$.
\end{definition}

Every quantum channel is PTP, but so is the transpose map and any composition of a channel with partial
transposition; this is why complete positivity is not assumed.

\begin{theorem}[Contractivity]\label{thm:contract}
If $\Phi$ is PTP and $X$ is Hermitian, then $\|\Phi X\|_1\le\|X\|_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $(P,Q)$ is a Jordan pair for $X$, then $(\Phi P,\Phi Q)$ is a Jordan pair for $\Phi X$ with the same
cost, since positivity is preserved and traces are unchanged. Hence $\|\Phi X\|_1$ is at most the cost of
every Jordan pair of $X$; take the infimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Branch contractivity]\label{thm:branch}
Let $\{\Psi_i\}_{i\in I}$ be a finite family of difference-respecting positive maps with
$\sum_i\tr\Psi_i(A)=\tr A$ for all $A\succeq0$. Then $\sum_{i}\|\Psi_iX\|_1\le\|X\|_1$ for Hermitian $X$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix a Jordan pair $(P,Q)$ for $X$. Each $(\Psi_iP,\Psi_iQ)$ is Jordan for $\Psi_iX$, so
$\|\Psi_iX\|_1\le\Rem(\tr\Psi_iP+\tr\Psi_iQ)$. Sum over $i$ and use the trace hypothesis twice to obtain
$\Rem(\tr P+\tr Q)$; take the infimum.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Duality}

\begin{definition}
A Hermitian $W$ is a \emph{contraction} if $\Id-W\succeq0$ and $\Id+W\succeq0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Weak duality]\label{thm:weak}
For Hermitian $X$ and any contraction $W$, $\Rem\tr(XW)\le\|X\|_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For a Jordan pair $(P,Q)$, Lemma~\ref{lem:tools} gives $\tr(P(\Id-W))\ge0$ and $\tr(Q(\Id+W))\ge0$, i.e.
$\Rem\tr(PW)\le\Rem\tr P$ and $-\Rem\tr(QW)\le\Rem\tr Q$. Add and use $X=P-Q$; take the infimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Sign operator]
For unitary $U$ and real $\lambda$, put $\sgn_U(\lambda)=U\diag(s(\lambda_i))U^{\dagger}$ where
$s(t)=+1$ for $t\ge0$ and $s(t)=-1$ for $t<0$.
\end{definition}

Since $\Id\mp\sgn_U(\lambda)=U\diag(1\mp s(\lambda_i))U^{\dagger}$ has non-negative entries in the
rotated basis, $\sgn_U(\lambda)$ is a contraction.

\begin{theorem}[Spectral formula]\label{thm:spectral}
If $X$ is Hermitian with eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n$ counted with multiplicity, then
$\|X\|_1=\sum_i|\lambda_i|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $X=U\diag(\lambda)U^{\dagger}$. The spectral Jordan pair has cost
$\sum_i\max(\lambda_i,0)+\sum_i\max(-\lambda_i,0)=\sum_i|\lambda_i|$, giving $\le$. Conversely
$W=\sgn_U(\lambda)$ is a contraction with $XW=U\diag(|\lambda_i|)U^{\dagger}$, so
$\Rem\tr(XW)=\sum_i|\lambda_i|$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:weak} gives $\ge$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strong duality; attainment]\label{thm:strong}
For every Hermitian $X$ there is a Jordan pair attaining the infimum and a contraction attaining the
supremum, and
\[
\|X\|_1=\min_{X=P-Q,\,P,Q\succeq0}\Rem(\tr P+\tr Q)
=\max_{-\Id\preceq W\preceq\Id}\Rem\tr(XW).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both optima are exhibited in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral}: the spectral Jordan pair and the
sign operator, each with value $\sum_i|\lambda_i|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Norm test for positivity]\label{prop:test}
If $X$ is Hermitian with $\|X\|_1\le\Rem\tr X$, then $X\succeq0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral}, $\sum_i|\lambda_i|\le\sum_i\lambda_i$, forcing $\lambda_i\ge0$ for all $i$.
\end{proof}

\section{Partial transposition}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{partial transpose} on the second factor is $(\Gamma X)_{(i,j),(k,l)}=X_{(i,l),(k,j)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:gamma}
$\Gamma$ is linear, an involution, trace preserving and Hermiticity preserving; $\Gamma(A\otimes B)
=A\otimes B^{T}$; and $\tr((\Gamma X)^2)=\tr(X^2)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Linearity and involutivity are immediate from the index formula, and diagonal entries are unchanged, so
the trace is preserved. Hermiticity: $\overline{(\Gamma X)_{(k,l),(i,j)}}=\overline{X_{(k,j),(i,l)}}
=X_{(i,l),(k,j)}=(\Gamma X)_{(i,j),(k,l)}$. The Kronecker formula is a direct computation. For the last
identity, expand $\tr(Y^2)=\sum_{u,v}Y_{uv}Y_{vu}$ and note that the index substitution defining
$\Gamma$ is an involutive bijection of the summation set.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$X$ is \emph{PPT} if $\Gamma X\succeq0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:kronpos}
If $A\succeq0$ and $B\succeq0$ then $A\otimes B\succeq0$, and $A\otimes B$ is PPT.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$A\otimes B=(A^{1/2}\otimes B^{1/2})^{\dagger}(A^{1/2}\otimes B^{1/2})\succeq0$, and
$\Gamma(A\otimes B)=A\otimes B^{T}$ with $B^{T}\succeq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local covariance]\label{thm:cov}
For all $A$ on $\HH_A$, $B$ on $\HH_B$ and all $X$,
\[
\Gamma\bigl((A\otimes B)X(A\otimes B)^{\dagger}\bigr)=(A\otimes\bar B)(\Gamma X)(A\otimes\bar B)^{\dagger}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand both sides entrywise. The left side at $((i,j),(k,l))$ equals
$\sum_{(a,b),(c,d)}A_{ia}B_{lb}X_{(a,b),(c,d)}\overline{A_{kc}}\,\overline{B_{jd}}$ after the index swap
of $\Gamma$. Reindexing by the involution $((a,b),(c,d))\mapsto((a,d),(c,b))$, a bijection of the
summation set, turns this into
$\sum_{(a,b),(c,d)}A_{ia}\overline{B_{jb}}(\Gamma X)_{(a,b),(c,d)}\overline{A_{kc}}B_{ld}$, which is the
corresponding entry of the right side.
\end{proof}

Thus partial transposition is equivariant under local conjugation, the sole cost being complex
conjugation of the factor acting on $B$. Since congruence preserves positivity, local conjugations
preserve the PPT property.

\section{Negativity and logarithmic negativity}

\begin{definition}
For a bipartite state $\rho$,
\[
\Neg(\rho)=\frac{\|\Gamma\rho\|_1-1}{2},\qquad \EN(\rho)=\log\|\Gamma\rho\|_1 .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\|\Gamma\rho\|_1\ge1$, hence $\Neg(\rho)\ge0$ and $\EN(\rho)\ge0$, for every state $\rho$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\Gamma\rho$ is Hermitian with trace $1$; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(2).
\end{proof}

If $\Gamma\rho$ has eigenvalues $\mu_i$ summing to $1$, then $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1=1+2\sum_{\mu_i<0}|\mu_i|$,
so $\Neg(\rho)$ is exactly the absolute weight of the negative part of the spectrum of $\Gamma\rho$.

\subsection{Monotonicity}

\begin{definition}
A map $\Lambda$ is a \emph{PPT operation} if it respects differences, maps positive matrices to positive
matrices, preserves the trace, and maps PPT matrices to PPT matrices.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Local operations are PPT operations]
If $(A\otimes B)^{\dagger}(A\otimes B)=\Id$ then $\Lambda(X)=(A\otimes B)X(A\otimes B)^{\dagger}$ is a
PPT operation.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Congruence preserves positivity; the trace is preserved by cyclicity and the isometry condition; and
Theorem~\ref{thm:cov} gives $\Gamma(\Lambda X)=(A\otimes\bar B)(\Gamma X)(A\otimes\bar B)^{\dagger}$,
which is positive whenever $\Gamma X$ is.
\end{proof}

Every LOCC protocol is a PPT operation, so monotonicity for PPT operations is strictly stronger.

\begin{lemma}[Conjugation]\label{lem:conj}
If $\Lambda$ is a PPT operation, then $\tilde\Lambda=\Gamma\circ\Lambda\circ\Gamma$ is PTP.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Differences are respected by both maps. If $A\succeq0$, then $\Gamma A$ is PPT because
$\Gamma\Gamma A=A\succeq0$; hence $\Lambda\Gamma A$ is PPT, i.e. $\Gamma\Lambda\Gamma A\succeq0$. Finally
$\tr\tilde\Lambda A=\tr\Lambda\Gamma A=\tr\Gamma A=\tr A$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
For a PPT operation $\Lambda$ and Hermitian $\rho$, $\|\Gamma(\Lambda\rho)\|_1\le\|\Gamma\rho\|_1$;
hence $\EN(\Lambda\rho)\le\EN(\rho)$ and $\Neg(\Lambda\rho)\le\Neg(\rho)$ for every state $\rho$.
In particular both are non-increasing under LOCC.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Gamma$ is an involution, so $\Gamma(\Lambda\rho)=\tilde\Lambda(\Gamma\rho)$ with $\tilde\Lambda$ PTP by
Lemma~\ref{lem:conj}; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:contract} to the Hermitian matrix $\Gamma\rho$ and then
monotonicity of $\log$ and of $t\mapsto(t-1)/2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Strong monotonicity and convexity}

\begin{definition}
A finite family $\{\Lambda_i\}$ is a \emph{PPT instrument} if each $\Lambda_i$ respects differences, maps
positive matrices to positive matrices and PPT matrices to PPT matrices, and $\sum_i\tr\Lambda_i(X)=\tr X$
for all $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:branch}
For a PPT instrument and Hermitian $\rho$, $\sum_i\|\Gamma(\Lambda_i\rho)\|_1\le\|\Gamma\rho\|_1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:branch} to $\Psi_i=\Gamma\circ\Lambda_i\circ\Gamma$, each positive and
difference-respecting by the argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:conj}, with correctly summing traces, and use
$\Gamma\Lambda_i\rho=\Psi_i(\Gamma\rho)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Weighted log inequality]\label{lem:jensen}
If $p_i>0$ with $\sum_ip_i=1$ and $t_i>0$, then $\sum_ip_i\log(t_i/p_i)\le\log\sum_it_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Jensen's inequality for the concave function $\log$ at the points $t_i/p_i$ with weights $p_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strong monotonicity]\label{thm:strongmono}
Let $\{\Lambda_i\}$ be a PPT instrument and $\rho$ a state with $p_i=\tr\Lambda_i\rho>0$ for all $i$,
and set $\rho_i=p_i^{-1}\Lambda_i\rho$. Then $\sum_ip_i\EN(\rho_i)\le\EN(\rho)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $t_i=\|\Gamma(\Lambda_i\rho)\|_1$. By homogeneity and linearity of $\Gamma$,
$\EN(\rho_i)=\log(t_i/p_i)$. Lemma~\ref{lem:jensen} bounds $\sum_ip_i\EN(\rho_i)$ by $\log\sum_it_i$,
and Lemma~\ref{lem:branch} bounds $\sum_it_i$ by $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convexity of the negativity]
For $w_i\ge0$ with $\sum_iw_i=1$ and states $\rho_i$,
$\Neg\bigl(\sum_iw_i\rho_i\bigr)\le\sum_iw_i\Neg(\rho_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Linearity of $\Gamma$ together with Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(3),(4) gives
$\|\Gamma\sum_iw_i\rho_i\|_1\le\sum_iw_i\|\Gamma\rho_i\|_1$; subtract $1=\sum_iw_i$ and halve.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Faithfulness on the PPT class}

\begin{theorem}[Faithfulness]\label{thm:faithful}
For a state $\rho$ the following are equivalent: $\rho$ is PPT; $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1=1$; $\EN(\rho)=0$;
$\Neg(\rho)=0$. Consequently $\EN(\rho)>0$ if and only if $\rho$ is not PPT.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\Gamma\rho\succeq0$ then $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1=\tr\Gamma\rho=1$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(1).
Conversely $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1=1=\Rem\tr\Gamma\rho$ forces $\Gamma\rho\succeq0$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:test}. The remaining equivalences hold because $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1\ge1$ and both
$\log$ and $t\mapsto(t-1)/2$ are strictly increasing and vanish at $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$\rho$ is \emph{separable} if $\rho=\sum_{i=1}^m w_i A_i\otimes B_i$ with $w_i\ge0$ and
$A_i,B_i\succeq0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
Every separable state is PPT, hence $\EN(\rho)=\Neg(\rho)=0$ for separable $\rho$. Equivalently,
$\EN(\rho)>0$ certifies entanglement.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Gamma(A_i\otimes B_i)=A_i\otimes B_i^{T}\succeq0$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:kronpos}, and a
non-negative combination of positive matrices is positive; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The implication is one-sided. Entangled PPT (bound entangled) states exist, and they have $\EN=0$:
the measure is faithful with respect to the PPT/non-PPT dichotomy, not the separable/entangled one.
\end{remark}

\section{The maximally entangled state and the dimension bound}

\begin{definition}
On $\HH\otimes\HH$ with $\dim\HH=d$, the \emph{swap} is $S_{(i,j),(k,l)}=[i=l][j=k]$, i.e.
$S(|x\rangle\otimes|y\rangle)=|y\rangle\otimes|x\rangle$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:swap}
$S$ is Hermitian with $S^2=\Id$, $S$ is a contraction, and $\|S\|_1=d^2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Hermiticity and $S^2=\Id$ are index computations. The matrices $\frac12(\Id\pm S)$ are Hermitian
idempotents, hence positive (a Hermitian idempotent $P$ satisfies $P=P^{\dagger}P$), so $S$ is a
contraction. The spectral Jordan pair of $S$ has cost $\tr\Id=d^2$, giving $\|S\|_1\le d^2$, while $S$
certifies itself: $\|S\|_1\ge\Rem\tr(S\cdot S)=\tr\Id=d^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{maximally entangled state} on $\HH\otimes\HH$, $\dim\HH=d$, is the rank-one projector onto
$d^{-1/2}\sum_i|i\rangle|i\rangle$, that is $(\Phi_d)_{(i,j),(k,l)}=\frac1d[i=j][k=l]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:phi}
$\Phi_d$ is a state, $\Gamma\Phi_d=\frac1dS$, and
\[
\|\Gamma\Phi_d\|_1=d,\qquad \EN(\Phi_d)=\log d,\qquad \Neg(\Phi_d)=\frac{d-1}{2}.
\]
In particular $\Phi_d$ is neither PPT nor separable when $d\ge2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(\Gamma\Phi_d)_{(i,j),(k,l)}=(\Phi_d)_{(i,l),(k,j)}=\frac1d[i=l][k=j]=\frac1dS_{(i,j),(k,l)}$; apply
homogeneity and Proposition~\ref{prop:swap}, then Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cs}
For Hermitian $X$ of size $N$, $\|X\|_1^2\le N\tr(X^2)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:spectral} and Cauchy--Schwarz,
$\bigl(\sum_i|\lambda_i|\bigr)^2\le N\sum_i\lambda_i^2=N\tr(X^2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dimension bound]\label{thm:dim}
For every state $\rho$ on $\HH_A\otimes\HH_B$,
$\|\Gamma\rho\|_1\le\sqrt{d_Ad_B}$ and $\EN(\rho)\le\frac12\log(d_Ad_B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:cs} with $N=d_Ad_B$ gives $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1^2\le d_Ad_B\tr((\Gamma\rho)^2)$, and
$\tr((\Gamma\rho)^2)=\tr(\rho^2)\le1$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:gamma} and the purity bound
$\sum_ir_i^2\le(\sum_ir_i)^2=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every state $\rho$ on $\mathbb{C}^d\otimes\mathbb{C}^d$, $\EN(\rho)\le\log d=\EN(\Phi_d)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Multiplicativity and additivity}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:kroncontract}
If $W$ and $V$ are contractions then so is $W\otimes V$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Hermiticity is clear, and bilinear expansion using $\Id\otimes\Id=\Id$ gives
\begin{align*}
\Id-W\otimes V&=\tfrac12\bigl((\Id-W)\otimes(\Id+V)+(\Id+W)\otimes(\Id-V)\bigr),\\
\Id+W\otimes V&=\tfrac12\bigl((\Id-W)\otimes(\Id-V)+(\Id+W)\otimes(\Id+V)\bigr).
\end{align*}
Each right-hand side is a positive combination of Kronecker products of positive matrices, hence
positive by Proposition~\ref{prop:kronpos}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicativity]\label{thm:mult}
For Hermitian $A$ and $B$, $\|A\otimes B\|_1=\|A\|_1\|B\|_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\le$) Let $(P_1,Q_1)$ and $(P_2,Q_2)$ be optimal Jordan pairs for $A$ and $B$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:strong}). Then
\[
A\otimes B=(P_1\otimes P_2+Q_1\otimes Q_2)-(P_1\otimes Q_2+Q_1\otimes P_2)
\]
is a Jordan pair by Proposition~\ref{prop:kronpos}, of cost
$(\tr P_1+\tr Q_1)(\tr P_2+\tr Q_2)=\|A\|_1\|B\|_1$.

($\ge$) Let $W$ and $V$ be optimal contractions for $A$ and $B$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:strong}). By
Lemma~\ref{lem:kroncontract}, $W\otimes V$ is a contraction, so Theorem~\ref{thm:weak} gives
\[
\|A\otimes B\|_1\ \ge\ \Rem\tr\bigl((AW)\otimes(BV)\bigr)=\Rem\bigl(\tr(AW)\tr(BV)\bigr)=\|A\|_1\|B\|_1,
\]
using that traces of products of two Hermitian matrices are real.
\end{proof}

If $\rho$ acts on $A_1B_1$ and $\sigma$ on $A_2B_2$, write $\rho\bx\sigma$ for $\rho\otimes\sigma$
regrouped across the cut $A_1A_2\mid B_1B_2$; regrouping is a permutation of basis vectors and preserves
positivity, traces and the trace norm.

\begin{lemma}
$\Gamma(\rho\bx\sigma)=(\Gamma\rho)\bx(\Gamma\sigma)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both sides transpose the $B_1$ and $B_2$ indices independently, as the composite index formula shows.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Additivity]\label{thm:add}
For states $\rho,\sigma$: $\|\Gamma(\rho\bx\sigma)\|_1=\|\Gamma\rho\|_1\|\Gamma\sigma\|_1$ and
$\EN(\rho\bx\sigma)=\EN(\rho)+\EN(\sigma)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the previous lemma with Theorem~\ref{thm:mult}; both factors are at least $1$, hence nonzero, so
the logarithm of the product splits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-additivity of the negativity]
For Hermitian $\rho,\sigma$,
$\Neg(\rho\bx\sigma)=2\Neg(\rho)\Neg(\sigma)+\Neg(\rho)+\Neg(\sigma)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1=2\Neg(\rho)+1$ and likewise for $\sigma$ into the multiplicative law and
simplify.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications: bounds on exact conversion}

\begin{theorem}[Exact distillation bound]\label{thm:dist}
Let $\rho$ be a state on $\mathbb{C}^{d_1}\otimes\mathbb{C}^{d_1}$ and $\sigma$ on
$\mathbb{C}^{d_2}\otimes\mathbb{C}^{d_2}$, and suppose a PPT operation $\Lambda$ satisfies
$\Lambda(\rho\bx\sigma)=\Phi_{d_1d_2}$. Then $\log(d_1d_2)\le\EN(\rho)+\EN(\sigma)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The output has $\EN=\log(d_1d_2)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:phi}; monotonicity (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}) and
additivity (Theorem~\ref{thm:add}) bound it by $\EN(\rho)+\EN(\sigma)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If a PPT operation maps $\rho\bx\rho$ exactly to the maximally entangled state of local dimension $d$,
then $\log d\le\EN(\rho)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[No exact distillation from PPT states]
Let $d\ge2$ and let $\rho$ be a PPT state on $\mathbb{C}^d\otimes\mathbb{C}^d$. Then no PPT operation
maps $\rho\bx\rho$ to $\Phi_d$, and none maps $\rho$ to $\Phi_d$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\EN(\rho)=0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}, so the previous corollary would force $\log d\le0$.
\end{proof}

This is the mechanism behind bound entanglement: since $\EN$ is additive, $n$ copies of a PPT state
still have $\EN=0$, so no exact PPT (in particular no LOCC) protocol extracts a maximally entangled pair.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Computing the monotone.} Given $\rho$ of size $d_Ad_B$: form $\Gamma\rho$ by the index
permutation (a reshape, an axis transposition and a reshape, $O(d_A^2d_B^2)$); diagonalise the Hermitian
matrix $\Gamma\rho$ ($O((d_Ad_B)^3)$); return $\|\Gamma\rho\|_1=\sum_i|\mu_i|$,
$\Neg=(\|\Gamma\rho\|_1-1)/2$, $\EN=\log\|\Gamma\rho\|_1$. The eigendecomposition dominates; no
optimisation over decompositions of the state is required, which is what makes the monotone practical.

\paragraph{Optimal certificate.} With $X=U\diag(\lambda)U^{\dagger}$, return $W=U\diag(s(\lambda))U^{\dagger}$
with $s(t)=\pm1$ according to the sign of $t$. Then $W^2=\Id$, so $-\Id\preceq W\preceq\Id$, and
$\Rem\tr(XW)=\|X\|_1$: a checkable certificate of the value of the norm, the analogue for the negativity
of an optimal entanglement witness.

\paragraph{Optimal splitting.} With the same data, $P=U\diag(\lambda^+)U^{\dagger}$ and
$Q=U\diag(\lambda^-)U^{\dagger}$ realise the primal minimum; at $X=\Gamma\rho$ the negativity equals
$\tr Q$, the operator onto which the failure of positivity is concentrated.

\section{Discussion}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Property of $\EN$ & Structural input\\
\midrule
Non-negativity & Trace bound $\Rem\tr X\le\|X\|_1$\\
Vanishing on PPT & $\|P\|_1=\tr P$ for $P\succeq0$\\
Faithfulness on the PPT class & Spectral formula (converse norm test)\\
Monotonicity under PPT operations & Contractivity under PTP maps\\
Strong monotonicity & Branch contractivity and Jensen\\
Convexity of $\Neg$ & Subadditivity and homogeneity\\
Dimension bound & Cauchy--Schwarz, purity, $\tr(\Gamma X)^2=\tr X^2$\\
Value at $\Phi_d$ & $\Gamma\Phi_d=S/d$ and self-certification of $S$\\
Additivity & Multiplicativity, which needs strong duality\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

All upper bounds come from the primal side and all lower bounds from the dual side; the two results
needing both --- the spectral formula and multiplicativity --- are exactly where attainment matters.

$\EN$ is not an entanglement entropy and is not asymptotically continuous, but it is computable,
additive and an upper bound on exact distillation rates, which makes it the standard tool for
certifying limits on entanglement manipulation in concrete finite systems. The trade-off with
faithfulness is intrinsic: no criterion computable from a single eigendecomposition can detect bound
entanglement, since deciding separability is hard in general.

The development is finite dimensional; the variational definition extends verbatim to trace-class
operators, but attainment of the dual optimum requires care with continuous spectrum. The monotonicity
theorems concern PPT operations, a class larger than LOCC, so they say nothing about the finer structure
of LOCC protocols. The applications concern exact conversion; the asymptotic theory needs continuity
estimates not developed here.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact duality formula for the monotone]
$\EN(\rho)=\log\max\{\Rem\tr(\Gamma\rho\,W): W \text{ Hermitian},\ -\Id\preceq W\preceq\Id\}$, and the
maximiser may always be taken of the form $\Gamma(\text{entanglement witness})$; consequently $\EN$
extends to a PPT-monotone functional on the whole real vector space of Hermitian operators that is
concave along the PPT boundary. Strong duality turns the monotone from an infimum into a maximum over a
compact convex set of certificates, so every lower bound on entanglement becomes an explicit
finite-dimensional witness.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Rigidity of the maximiser]
On $\mathbb{C}^d\otimes\mathbb{C}^d$, $\EN(\rho)=\log d$ only for maximally entangled states: then
$\rho=(U\otimes V)\Phi_d(U\otimes V)^{\dagger}$ for local unitaries $U,V$. The Cauchy--Schwarz step is an
equality only when all $|\lambda_i|$ coincide and the purity step only for pure states; chaining the two
equality cases forces the partial transpose to have flat spectrum $\pm1/d$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Asymptotic distillation and irreversibility]
For every state $\rho$ and every $n,m$ admitting an exact PPT protocol mapping $\rho^{\otimes n}$ to
$\Phi_{2^m}$, one has $m\log2\le n\EN(\rho)$; moreover there exist PPT-entangled states whose
entanglement cost under PPT operations is strictly positive while this distillation rate is $0$, so the
PPT theory of entanglement is irreversible.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Continuity and asymptotic robustness]
$\EN$ is continuous on states with an explicit modulus of continuity in terms of trace distance and
dimension, so the exact-conversion bounds extend to $\varepsilon$-approximate conversions with an
additive error vanishing as $\varepsilon\to0$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Instrument-level rigidity]
Equality in $\sum_ip_i\EN(\rho_i)=\EN(\rho)$ for a PPT instrument holds only when the branch maps
preserve the spectral projections of the positive and negative parts of $\Gamma\rho$; the equality cases
of Jensen's inequality and of the branch inequality together force each branch to inherit an optimal
Jordan pair.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The logarithmic negativity is best understood as the logarithm of the value of a semidefinite program.
The primal reading --- minimise $\tr P+\tr Q$ over splittings $\Gamma\rho=P-Q$ --- makes monotonicity
under positivity- and trace-preserving processing automatic, because such processing maps splittings to
splittings at unchanged cost. The dual reading --- maximise $\Rem\tr(\Gamma\rho\,W)$ over the operator
interval --- supplies certificates, hence lower bounds, hence the exact multiplicativity that turns
subadditivity into additivity. The two readings meet, with both optima attained explicitly by spectral
data, and the resulting monotone is non-negative, vanishing exactly on the PPT states, non-increasing
under LOCC even selectively, convex in the un-logged variable, additive under tensor products, bounded
by $\frac12\log(d_Ad_B)$, and maximal precisely at the maximally entangled state.

